  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe forecast area will be under the influence of broad ridging\naloft through most of the weekend with dry conditions and\nslightly above normal highs. Highs for today and Sunday will\nmainly be in the 50s but we could see a few lower 60s today\nacross southern New Hampshire or southwest coastal Maine. \n\nWe transition to southwest flow aloft on Sunday as the ridge\naxis pushes east. A trough will start to approach from the west\nas a surface low begins to deepen off the coast of the \nCarolina's. This low will gradually ride up the coast through\nthe day on Sunday which will lead to increasing rain chances\nfrom southwest to northeast as the better forcing for ascent\narrives late in the day or in the evening. We are then likely \nto see widespread high chances (80 to 95+ percent) of rain \nspread across all of New Hampshire and western Maine. \nYesterday's 12z NBM data had shifted the highest probabilities \nfor totals of an inch or greater a bit south and east, but the \n18z runs shifted everything back west and north. Thus, we still \nhave some issues to iron out before confidence increases with \nregards to eventual track. That being said, these probabilities \ncurrently range from around 40 percent over far northern New \nHampshire, to the 50-70 percent range across northwest Maine, to\nthe 80-95+ percent range over southern New Hampshire and \ncoastal/interior Maine. The highest probabilities paint the \nbest chances for an inch or greater over coastal Maine and \nvicinity. Increasing the threshold to 2 inches, and the highest \nprobabilities range from around 40 to 60 percent across southern\nNew Hampshire and coastal/interior Maine. Again, the \nprobabilities are maximized around coastal Maine and vicinity. \n\nA cold front will cross the forecast area early in the day on\nMonday will will lead to winds shifting from out of the\nsoutheast to out of the northwest, becoming breezy by the \nafternoon hours. GFS soundings suggest the potential for some\nsites to see 25 to 35 knots at the top of the mixed layer.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nConfidence continues to increase that the active pattern may \nwell last into mid week towards the Thanksgiving holiday as \nlong range guidance signals the chance for additional low \npressure systems organizing over the eastern CONUS and moving \nup the coast. A wide range of solutions remain possible for this\nnext system, but hopefully we will get a better idea once the\nSunday night/Monday system moves out. Thus far, confidence\nremains low, but the storm may overlap with the monthly high \nastronomical tide cycle. The high tides will make the coast \nsusceptible to coastal flooding if combined with swell and surge\nfrom a coastal low. Those with interests along the coast should\nmonitor coming forecasts should this threat become more likely.\nLooking ahead, the active pattern may continue.",
